article_body_html: |
  <p><strong>[ This is the reusable post template. Replace the copy, keep the structure. ]</strong> Two quotes for the same court can differ by forty per cent and both can be technically accurate. The gap is almost never the surface material — it is what each contractor has assumed about the ground underneath.</p>

  <h2>1. The build-up section</h2>
  <p>Any quote worth reading names the layers from the top down, with a thickness against each. If your quote says "supply and lay artificial turf" and nothing else, you are not comparing surfaces — you are comparing optimism.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Pile height and dtex for turf, coat count for acrylic</li>
    <li>Shock pad thickness, if any</li>
    <li>Binder course and sub-base depth</li>
    <li>Compaction density the contractor will achieve</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. What is excluded</h2>
  <p>Exclusions belong in the same type size as inclusions. The usual quiet omissions are excavation, disposal of spoil, dewatering, and drainage connection to your existing storm line.</p>

  <blockquote><p>If the exclusions are in smaller type than the inclusions, read them twice.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>3. The drainage line item</h2>
  <p>Look for a cross fall figure and a perimeter drain. A quote with no drainage line on an open-ground site is a quote that intends to raise a variation, or one that has not visited the site.</p>

